## Packages and Rust host functions

Use a source package for reusable Moth code and a host package for functions or
resources implemented outside Moth. Both enter normal package resolution.
Neither grants special access to another module's private implementation.

### Provide a source package

`SourcePackage::new` assigns the public package root. `from_dir` supplies its
installed files. Registration through `BuilderDefinition::source_package`
records Builder provenance without asking the caller to construct an internal
registry entry.

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    let package = SourcePackage::new("@ui")
        .from_dir("installed-packages/ui")
        .available_in_templates();
]

`available_in_templates` makes the package's exported constants eligible for
implicit template scope. It doesn't inject arbitrary Rust functions, expose
private constants or change Moth's no-shadowing rules. Ambiguous visible names
are errors.

Packages compile in their own semantic boundaries under the selected numeric
profile. Builder source packages don't inherit the application's private
project globals. Project-specific compile-time inputs use an explicitly
registered synthetic interface with tracked provenance, not ambient state.

### Derive a host interface from a Rust signature

Apply `#[moth::host_function]` to a supported concrete Rust free function. Add
its generated descriptor to a package explicitly:

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    let package = HostPackage::new("@platform")
        .function(moth::host_binding!(has_prefix));
]

The attribute preserves the Rust function and generates a typed binding
description and call adapter. `host_binding!` selects that generated
description. Package registration gives it its Moth package and symbol
identity. The normal function name is the default exposed name.

The attribute removes duplicate signature declarations. It doesn't register a
global plugin, scan the crate for other functions or create an HTTP endpoint.
Rust `pub` visibility and Moth package visibility remain separate from network
exposure. The package definition is the place to inspect which functions the
builder provides.

Generated descriptions enter the same canonical registration path as manual
host descriptors. The macro generates no Moth source to parse again and
introduces no alternate semantic type system. Manual descriptors receive the
same signature, identity and target validation. They cannot make an invalid
Rust adapter safe by claiming a different signature.

### The automatic binding profile

The automatic adapter accepts a closed set of Rust signature shapes. Each has
one Moth meaning. These are boundary rules, not a claim that Rust and Moth have
the same layout or ownership model.

[table:
    [th:
        [: Rust shape]
        [: Moth meaning]
    ]
    [td:
        [: `bool` and `char`]
        [: `Bool` and `Char`]
    ]
    [td:
        [: `i8`, `i16`, `i32`, `i64`]
        [: The matching fixed-width signed type]
    ]
    [td:
        [: `u8`, `u16`, `u32`, `u64`]
        [: The matching fixed-width unsigned type]
    ]
    [td:
        [: `f32` and `f64`]
        [: `F32` and `F64`, with Moth's finite-value checks]
    ]
    [td:
        [: `moth::host::Byte`]
        [: Nominal `Byte`, distinct from `U8`]
    ]
    [td:
        [: `moth::host::Int` and `moth::host::Float`]
        [: Profile-selected `Int` and `Float`]
    ]
    [td:
        [: `&str` parameter]
        [: Shared string input with call-scoped Rust access]
    ]
    [td:
        [: `String` result]
        [: An independent Moth string result]
    ]
    [td:
        [: `Option<T>` for an eligible value type]
        [: One optional Moth value]
    ]
    [td:
        [: `()` as the return type]
        [: Zero success result slots]
    ]
    [td:
        [: `Result<T, moth::host::Error>` as the outer return type]
        [: The success shape plus the built-in Moth error channel]
    ]
    [td:
        [: `&T` or `&mut T` for an explicitly registered host resource]
        [: Shared or exclusive access to that opaque resource]
    ]
]

Rust `i32` means `I32`, even when Moth's selected `Int` happens to be 32 bits.
Rust `u8` means `U8`, never `Byte`. The host wrappers express those distinct
Moth types without asking the adapter to guess from a machine word size.

`Result<(), moth::host::Error>` has zero success slots and an error channel.
`Result<Option<T>, moth::host::Error>` keeps absence distinct from failure.
This mapping doesn't create a source-level Result object or tuple in Moth.
The error's code is a `U32` value. Code zero is valid error data and doesn't
turn an error outcome into success.

The automatic profile rejects architecture-dependent `usize` and `isize`, raw
pointers, borrowed results, arbitrary Rust references, unregistered structs,
trait objects, generic functions, `impl Trait`, async functions and callbacks.
Arrays, collections, tuples and arbitrary nested Rust Result values acquire no
implicit mapping. A type accepted elsewhere in Moth isn't automatically part
of this host profile. Write a concrete wrapper with an eligible surface when a
Rust API uses another representation.

A Rust type alias doesn't bypass these restrictions. Generated type obligations
let Rust check the resolved type and adapter implementation. The procedural
macro examines syntax rather than resolving arbitrary Rust names or analysing
function bodies. It doesn't infer purity, absence of panics or application
permissions from a function signature.

### Values cross through an adapter

The runtime adapter validates scalar encodings, numeric ranges, finite floats,
Unicode and string lengths before exposing a value at the receiving boundary.
Invalid values don't become successful Moth results through truncation,
sentinel substitution or a changed type. A host contract violation is a
boundary fault, not an undeclared Moth error return.

A Rust `&str` parameter grants access only for that call. The adapter may copy
into call-owned storage when the runtime cannot safely provide a borrowed view.
It promises correct values and lifetimes, not zero-copy access. Host code cannot
retain a reference into guest memory after the call.

String results become independent Moth storage. Rust allocator ownership,
reference counts and raw pointers don't pass through as Moth ownership. The
adapter follows the declared ABI and cleanup contract rather than transmuting
Rust layouts. A Rust `repr(C)` annotation alone supplies none of that contract.

A recoverable Rust failure is converted deliberately to `moth::host::Error` by
the wrapper. The attribute doesn't stringify every Rust error, catch every
panic or expose sensitive internal details automatically. Assertions, traps,
resource exhaustion and faults outside the typed result remain distinct from
normal Moth error returns.

### Register opaque resources

Use `#[moth::host_type]` for a Rust-owned resource whose internals stay on the
host. Register its type and free-function surface in the package:

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    #[moth::host_type]
    struct Window {
        width: u32,
        height: u32,
    }

    #[moth::host_function]
    fn resize(window: &mut Window, width: u32, height: u32) {
        window.width = width;
        window.height = height;
    }

    let package = HostPackage::new("@window")
        .opaque_type::<Window>()
        .function(moth::host_binding!(resize));
]

The annotation describes an opaque Moth type, not a serialisable Rust struct.
Its fields, native layout and address remain private to Rust. Registering the
type creates no resource instance. The host resource API supplies instances
under an explicit lifecycle and resolves their handles when functions run.

A resource handle is associated with its registered type, owning runtime and
lifecycle. The adapter rejects the wrong type, a stale handle or a handle from
another runtime before constructing a Rust reference. Repeated aliases also
remain subject to access checks. Two handles do not prove two distinct mutable
resources.

`&mut Window` requires exclusive resource access. It doesn't mean that an
arbitrary request is authorised or that the function cannot mutate unrelated
host state. Host-side calls and nested invocations obey the resource access
contract as well as Moth's static checks.

The package exports free functions. The attribute doesn't manufacture Moth
receiver methods or expose every Rust method on the resource. Moth source
wrappers can provide a higher-level API through ordinary source-owned types.

### Supply host context explicitly

A host-context parameter is supplied by the runtime rather than by the Moth
caller. Mark it explicitly and keep it separate from ordinary parameters:

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    struct HostState {
        user_name: Option<String>,
    }

    #[moth::host_function]
    fn current_user(
        #[moth(context)] host: &HostState,
    ) -> Option<String> {
        host.user_name.clone()
    }
]

The Moth function takes no argument and returns an optional string. The clone
provides an independent result rather than retaining a borrow of host state.
The runtime supplies the required `HostState` for the invocation. An absent or
incompatible context is rejected before calling the Rust function.

A binding has at most one marked context parameter. Context is neither a
serialised Moth value nor an arbitrary dependency-injection registry. A mutable
context has an exclusive access requirement. The host determines its scope,
such as an application, session or request, and never shares request-private
state accidentally through a global instance.

### Install the execution surface

Compilation consumes package descriptions. Execution consumes the matching
runtime adapters. The embedding host installs implementations for the import
contract recorded on the Wasm artefact and supplies the required resource and
context owners. Missing implementations or mismatched contracts fail before
normal execution.

A native Rust implementation isn't a JavaScript implementation. Browser and
server targets require their own compatible bindings. The SDK doesn't turn a
missing browser binding into RPC. A host import is also not a public web route.
Authentication, authorisation and HTTP mutation protection belong to the web
builder and application boundary that receive the request.

Host-binding attributes are the SDK's narrowly scoped use of procedural
macros. They generate checked descriptions and adapters. They don't provide an
extension point for arbitrary compiler passes or unrelated metaprogramming.
